+# Pitch 01 — condo moisture and sound
+
+**Targets:** Toronto condo and real-estate verticals, property-management
+newsletters, design blogs that cover downtown units.
+
+**Why this angle lands:** it is a warning, not an offer. Every condo board in the
+city is already arguing about flooring, and nobody has written the technical
+piece that explains *why* the two failure modes are the two failure modes.
+
+**What we can put behind it, all already published:**
+
+- `/papers/toronto-hardwood-climate-moisture-protocol` — the RH range and the
+  test methods, with the failure modes named
+- `/guides/reference-condominium-concrete-slab` — a complete published
+  specification for exactly this scenario
+- `/standards` — ASTM F2170 and F1869 with last-verified dates, which is the
+  part a journalist can check
+- `/framework` — a published, versioned, citable standard they can quote against
+
+---
+
+**Subject:** The two ways condo hardwood fails in Toronto, and why boards keep
+seeing the same complaint
+
+Hi [Name],
+
+Two failure modes account for most hardwood complaints in Toronto towers, and
+they are not the ones the flooring ads talk about.
+
+The first is the slab. A concrete floor in a finished unit can still be releasing
+moisture years after occupancy, and the test that detects it — in-situ relative
+humidity probes, ASTM F2170 — is not the surface reading most installers do. A
+floor laid over an untested slab is fine until the first humid August.
+
+The second is the assembly, not the plank. Acoustic performance is a property of
+the whole build-up — underlay, adhesive, subfloor — and a board's declaration is
+written against the assembly. Buying a plank rated in someone else's assembly
+proves nothing about yours.
+
+We publish the full protocol for both, free and citable:
+
+- The moisture protocol, with the test methods named:
+  https://ecowoods.ca/papers/toronto-hardwood-climate-moisture-protocol
+- A complete reference specification for a concrete-slab condo:
+  https://ecowoods.ca/guides/reference-condominium-concrete-slab
+- The standards register, with what each one governs and when we last checked
+  the edition: https://ecowoods.ca/standards
+
+What I can offer your readers, at no cost and with no plug required:
+
+- A live slab-meter demonstration, on camera, showing the difference between a
+  surface reading and an in-situ probe
+- A cutaway of a real assembly against a box-store equivalent
+- A technical review of any piece you are already writing — happy to be a
+  fact-check rather than a source
+
+We publish a scoring framework anyone can run against any contractor's quote,
+including ours: https://ecowoods.ca/framework/assess — that is the part I would
+most like your readers to have, whoever they end up hiring.
+
+[Name]
+Ecowoods Inc. · Toronto
+(647) 244-5156 · services@ecowoods.ca
