+# Pitch 02 — heritage floors and the sanding that cannot be undone
+
+**Targets:** heritage and architectural-conservation outlets, Toronto
+neighbourhood associations, real-estate desks covering the older west and east
+ends, renovation media.
+
+**Why this angle lands:** it is about irreversible loss, and it is checkable. A
+wear layer is finite and measurable, and the arithmetic is the story.
+
+**What we can put behind it:**
+
+- `/papers/hardwood-refinishing-machines-and-sequence` — the four machines, what
+  each removes, and what a skipped stage leaves behind
+- `/glossary/wear-layer` — the term, defined, and why the number is finite
+- `/guides/reference-refinishing-existing-hardwood` — the published spec
+- `/library` — the diagrams, each linked to the page that explains it
+
+---
+
+**Subject:** A heritage floor can be sanded a fixed number of times. Most owners
+find out afterwards.
+
+Hi [Name],
+
+There is a number nobody tells homeowners with a hundred-year-old floor: how
+many times it can still be sanded.
+
+Every full sand removes material. The wear layer above the tongue is finite, and
+once it is gone the floor is not restorable at any price — it is replaceable.
+A contractor who opens with 36 grit on a floor that needed a screen and recoat
+has spent a decade of that floor's remaining life in an afternoon, and the
+result looks *better* on handover day. That is what makes it hard to see.
+
+The distinction is not subtle to anyone in the trade, and it is completely
+invisible to the person paying:
+
+- **Screen and recoat** abrades the finish only. The wood is untouched.
+- **Full sand** goes to bare wood, and costs material that does not come back.
+
+We publish the whole sequence — the four machines, the grit progression, and
+what each stage can and cannot fix:
+https://ecowoods.ca/papers/hardwood-refinishing-machines-and-sequence
+
+For a piece on heritage housing stock, I can offer:
+
+- A measured wear-layer cross-section from a real Toronto floor, photographed
+- The two-question test that tells an owner which service they actually need,
